// Practice quiz: Constructor injection across layers
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/porting-and-configuration/constructor-injection-across-layers

::o1 no autowired::A class has one constructor with one parameter. What annotation does it need for constructor injection? {
~@Autowired on the field#Not quite. That is field injection.
~@Bean on the constructor#Not quite. @Bean belongs on methods.
~@Value on the parameter#Not quite. @Value binds configuration values.
=None#Correct. A single constructor is used automatically.
}

::o1 final::What does a final field prove about a constructor-injected dependency? {
~It is a singleton#Not quite. Scope is separate from final.
=It is set exactly once, during construction#Correct. It cannot be missing or reassigned later.
~It is thread-safe in every way#Not quite. The object it refers to may still be mutable.
~It has no null value at compile time#Not quite. The compiler does not prove that.
}

::o2 fake repository::What does a hand-written fake repository do in a service unit test? {
=Implements the interface with simple in-memory behavior#Correct. It lets the test control data without a database.
~Starts an embedded database#Not quite. That would be an integration test.
~Replaces the service#Not quite. The service is what is under test.
~Requires @MockitoBean#Not quite. A plain fake needs no Spring.
}

::o2 test speed::Why is a plain unit test of a service fast? {
~It uses a faster JVM#Not quite. The JVM is the same.
~It skips assertions#Not quite. Assertions still run.
=It starts no application context#Correct. It only runs your class and a fake.
~It compiles less code#Not quite. Compilation is not the main cost.
}

::o3 swapping implementation::How does depending on an interface help when replacing an in-memory repository with JPA? {
~The JPA class extends the service#Not quite. Repositories do not extend services.
=The service does not change#Correct. Only the bean behind the interface changes.
~Spring generates the service again#Not quite. Spring does not regenerate your service.
~The controller calls JPA directly instead#Not quite. That would break the layering.
}

